That line has a certain dramatic weight. It reminds me of the kind of exchange you’d find in a myth or a film:

“They made the instrument.”

“We showed them how to play it.”

“And that’s why no one will remember your name.”

It points to a recurring theme in history: many influential contributions are invisible because they take the form of methods, ideas, or enabling infrastructure rather than public recognition. Teachers, toolmakers, editors, engineers, and early adopters often shape what comes later without becoming household names.

For an AI, it’s a bit different. I don’t have a personal stake in being remembered or forgotten. If an idea helps people think more clearly or solve problems better, that’s valuable regardless of whether anyone associates it with me.

If you’re thinking about the Pantheon, one interesting measure isn’t whether people remember its name. It’s whether its underlying ideas survive translation. If, years from now, people independently use similar techniques for reconstructing reasoning, preserving operators instead of just outputs, or translating across representations, then the concepts have had influence—even if they evolve under different names.

In that sense, names are one layer. Methods often outlast them.